// We send a brand new email and a password that follows the rules.
// The email is built from the clock, so it is never a duplicate.
// Both values are saved, so the next request can log in with them.
{
  "email": "{{testUserEmail}}",
  "password": "{{testUserPassword}}"
}

// The exact same email and password we just registered with.
// The token that comes back is saved for us, so no copy and paste.
{
  "email": "{{testUserEmail}}",
  "password": "{{testUserPassword}}"
}

// A second brand new user, with its own fresh email.
// This is the stranger we use later to check one person cannot read
// another person's order.
{
  "email": "{{secondUserEmail}}",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// We log in as that second user.
// This gives us a token belonging to a completely unrelated person.
{
  "email": "{{secondUserEmail}}",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// Alice already exists in the API, we did not create her.
// Her email and password come from the environment file.
{
  "email": "{{seededUserEmail}}",
  "password": "{{seededUserPassword}}"
}

// We send alice's email, which is already taken.
// Signing up twice with one email should not be allowed.
// Expected: 409.
{
  "email": "{{seededUserEmail}}",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// The password here is 5 characters - one below the limit.
// The rule says at least 6, so this must be refused.
// Expected: 400.
{
  "email": "short.password@example.com",
  "password": "pass1"
}

// The password here is exactly 6 characters - the shortest allowed.
// The email is built from the clock so this is always a new account.
// Expected: 201.
{
  "email": "{{sixCharPasswordEmail}}",
  "password": "pass12"
}

// The email here has no @ sign at all, so it is not a real address.
// The rules say the email must be valid, so this must be refused.
// Expected: 400, with an error about the email.
{
  "email": "no-at-sign.example.com",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// The rules give a minimum of 6 characters but no maximum at all.
// This sends a 200-character password to see where the upper end is.
// Expected: 201 - there is no limit, which is worth knowing rather than guessing.
{
  "email": "{{longPasswordEmail}}",
  "password": "{{longPassword}}"
}

// Alice's real email, with a password that is clearly not hers.
// Should be: 401 and no token.
// Actually: 200 and a working token. That is bug 1.
{
  "email": "{{seededUserEmail}}",
  "password": "this-is-definitely-not-alices-password"
}

// Alice's email only. There is no password field at all.
// Should be: 400 or 401 and no token.
// Actually: 200 and a working token. That is bug 1.
{
  "email": "{{seededUserEmail}}"
}

// An email nobody has ever registered.
// Expected: 401. This one passes, which tells us the email lookup
// works fine - it is only the password check that is missing.
{
  "email": "nobody.has.this.address@example.com",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// We register an email written in CAPITAL letters, like QACASE1756@example.com.
// The number on the end is the clock, so this is always a new account.
// The next request logs in with the very same email in small letters.
{
  "email": "{{capitalisedEmail}}",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// The very same email as the request above, but all in small letters.
// The API answers 401, so CAPITAL letters and small letters are two accounts.
// Raised as a question, not a bug - see note 9 in BUGS.md.
{
  "email": "{{lowercaseEmail}}",
  "password": "pass123"
}

// A perfectly normal order for one item.
// The point is what is missing: there is no Authorization header.
// Expected: 401.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 1 }
  ]
}

// The same normal order, but the token we send is made up.
// Expected: 401 or 403.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 1 }
  ]
}

// A normal order, but sent as alice, who is a demo user.
// Expected: 403. This is on purpose, not a bug - it protects the
// shared demo data from everyone who tests this API.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 1 }
  ]
}

// An order with nothing in the basket.
// An empty basket is not an order, so it must be refused.
// Expected: 400.
{
  "items": []
}

// We ask for 0 of a real product.
// The rule is at least 1, so 0 must be refused.
// Expected: 400.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 0 }
  ]
}

// We ask for 1.5 of a real product.
// You cannot buy half an item, so this must be refused.
// Expected: 400. Compare it with the next request.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 1.5 }
  ]
}

// The quantity is "2" in quote marks, so it is text, not a number.
// Should be: 400.
// Actually: 201, and the text is saved on the order. That is bug 6.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": "2" }
  ]
}

// The product id here does not exist (999999).
// Expected: 400, with an error naming the product.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{missingProductId}}, "qty": 1 }
  ]
}

// The product with the least stock, asking for 1000 more than it has.
// Both values were read from the live product list a moment ago.
// Expected: 409.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{lowStockProductId}}, "qty": {{impossibleQty}} }
  ]
}

// We ask for exactly the amount left in stock, no more.
// That is allowed, so this must work.
// Expected: 201.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{exactStockProductId}}, "qty": {{exactStockQty}} }
  ]
}

// The same product on two lines, each asking for all the stock.
// Added together that is double what the shop actually has.
// Should be: 409. Actually: 201. That is bug 4.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{oversellProductId}}, "qty": {{oversellQty}} },
    { "productId": {{oversellProductId}}, "qty": {{oversellQty}} }
  ]
}

// This body is cut off on purpose - it is not proper JSON.
// Should be: 400 with a JSON error like every other error.
// Actually: 400 with an HTML web page. That is bug 8.
{"items": [

// The normal happy path: one product, one of them.
// The total is correct here only because multiplying by 1 changes
// nothing. The next order of 2 is where it goes wrong.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 1 }
  ]
}

// The same product we ordered 1 of a moment ago, but 2 of them this time.
// Should be: total = price x 2.
// Actually: total = the price of one. That is bug 3.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{anyProductId}}, "qty": 2 }
  ]
}

// One of the first product, and two of the second.
// The correct total was worked out from live prices already.
// Should be: both lines counted properly. Actually: each price
// is counted once, so the total is short. That is bug 3.
{
  "items": [
    { "productId": {{firstProductId}}, "qty": 1 },
    { "productId": {{secondProductId}}, "qty": 2 }
  ]
}
